00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.140
So what is Firebase?

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.570
On a higher level,
Firebase is Google Cloud's

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.710
specialized mobile and web
app development platform.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.940
Let me give you a little
bit of background.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.440
If you come here to any of
the Google Cloud talk today,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.780
you probably already know
that the Google Cloud

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.370
is a powerful tool.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.640
It is built on cutting-edge
technologies in both hardware

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.410
and software infrastructure.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.110
It is also platform agnostic
because no matter what

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.750
platform your games are
at, GCP can support it.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.440
It can be your
backbone because you

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.820
remove the burden of
provisioning and managing

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:14.990
your own server.

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:18.580
And it provides a variety of
tools to help you to scale.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:23.350
So tools like App Engine, Cloud
Storage, BigQuery, TensorFlow

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.130
and many, many other tools.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:26.510
It's very powerful.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.890
And it provides a reliable
and secure connection

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.390
so you can safely deploy
your code to the cloud.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.920
So what is Firebase?

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.670
Firebase is built
on top of the GCP.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.610
And it is specialized in
mobile and web applications.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.360
So if you're building a
mobile game or web app,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.270
Firebase is more tailored
to your experiences

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.330
because we focus on many of the
common problems, engineering

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.870
problems that mobile
developers facing.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.940
And also we provide custom
APIs so your app can easily

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.760
communicate with the GCP,
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.220
So in a way that would
provide quicker path to get

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.120
you started on GCP.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.760
So here's a subset of
the Firebase products

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.190
that we are going talk
to you about today.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.990
As you can see, Firebase
deliver solutions

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:18.460
throughout the entire lifecycle,
from building a game, improve

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.250
the quality, to
engage more users,

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.800
and driving player retention.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.900
And no matter what
you team size is,

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.100
it could be one person startup
or enterprise-level companies,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.730
we have tools that can
support any use cases.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:37.540
And our products also focus on
many aspects of the business.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.450
If you're engineer-- engineer
like to focus on problem like,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.520
how do I do this
more efficiently?

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.525
Or how do I scale?

00:03:44.525 --> 00:03:45.650
So we have tools like that.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.900
So, for example, if you're
sending push notifications,

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.027
we have Cloud Messaging.

00:03:49.027 --> 00:03:50.860
And then if you want
to do [INAUDIBLE] flow,

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.420
we have authentication.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.960
Or if you wonder, how do I build
a serverless infrastructure,

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.040
we have GCP tools like
Cloud Storage and Firestore.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.080
And these are the
critical components

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.160
of building a game app.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.510
And that's why the
engineers was focused on.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.730
And Firebase provide
tools so that you can--

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:10.750
help you to save--

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.860
and save the cost.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:13.900
And it also help you
to build infrastructure

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.529
so you don't have to do that.

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:18.660
If you're a product
manager, you wonder,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.160
how does my game perform?

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.220
You want to understand
your users better.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.760
So we have tools
like that as well,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.810
like Analytics, A/B
testing, or BigQuery.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:32.400
A lot of our products can
export BigQuery easily

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.510
so you can do deep
analysis on GCP.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:39.900
And you can also run experiments
to understand your users better

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.270
while making the
product decisions.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.910
And lastly, if you
have QA team, we've

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:48.480
got great tools there to cover
like any aspects of improving

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.080
your apps quality, like
Crashlytics, Performance,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.700
and Test Lab.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.530
Later in the session,
Sumit and Patrick

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.440
will do a deep dive on
some of the use case

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.420
to show how exactly
you can do this.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.000
And what's best about
Firebase and GCP

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.820
is that they work well together.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.820
Under the hood, they have the
same project and same billing

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:11.570
account.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:14.250
So it's very easy
for you to manage.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:15.810
And if you're a
Cloud customer, you

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.920
can import your project
to Firebase instantly.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.390
And many of our
Firebase products

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:24.610
are already running on GCP, such
as Cloud Storage, Firestore,

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:27.170
and Cloud Functions, so
anything with a cloud.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.740
So say you're a first-time user.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.230
What do you get outside
this Firebase package?

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.060
Let me help you to viralize it.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.190
So first you get
Firebase console.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.840
It serves as a
developer control panel.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.940
It's where you set
up a new project.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:46.800
And you can configure and
monitor your Firebase services.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.550
And then you get a bunch of SDK.

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.190
You pick and choose
the one you need.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:51.600
You install in your game.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.170
And you run a bunch of code to
quickly enable functionalities.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.300
And the client will
make secure connections

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.160
to the Firebase service on
the Cloud and to this console.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.170
So it will all sync up together.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.230
And the best part is
you don't have to build

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.100
any of the infrastructure.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.277
And it is secure.

00:06:07.277 --> 00:06:07.860
It's scalable.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.270
Oh, and one more thing.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.760
Firebase, the console
is also great tool

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:16.830
for your game designers
or marketing people

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.795
if they want like monitor
data or added game contents.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.560
And we'll provide
a variety of SDKs.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.060
For Android developers
here, the game developer,

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.990
we have SuperPass and
Unity cross-platform SDKs.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:35.710
And if you are iOS and
Android developer like me,

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.560
we have that as well.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.170
And lastly, our web SDKs
supports both mobile and web

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:42.388
applications.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.450
Firebase was
launched since 2016.

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:51.020
And over three years, we've
seen tremendous growth.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:56.210
To this day, we have over 1.5
million monthly active apps.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.370
And developers choosing
us because, A, Firebase

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.140
is easy to use, it's powered
by Google Cloud technology,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:05.490
and integrates well with many
cross-platform and third-party

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:05.990
tools.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.320
Now I have some new
updates for you.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.690
If you're a Unity developer,
now the onboarding flow

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.100
has become easier for you.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.720
Previously, when you set up a
Unity cross-platform project,

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.220
you had to set it up twice.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.930
You had to go to
the iOS platform,

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.300
set it up, and do
it again on Android.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.470
Now we get this
shiny Unity button.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:32.650
If you click on it,
now it can set up

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:36.770
both your iOS and Android
app at the same time

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.240
and download the
configuration files and import

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.480
to your project and finish
with downloading the Unity SDK.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:45.800
Again, it's quick and easy.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.270
Lastly, I want to talk
a bit about open source.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.750
At Firebase, we really value
the transparency of our SDK.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.420
We understand that it is
important for the developers

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.870
to know the exact behavior
of our source code,

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:03.420
especially when it comes to
how we handle the user privacy

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.440
and data, which is why we're
excited that our C++ SDK is

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.970
also open source now.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:12.720
Now if you're a C++ developer,
you can choose to integrate one

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.790
of our open-source SDK.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:16.290
With the source
code accessible, it

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:19.390
provides great flexibility
in your development flow.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.450
First, you can audit
your source code

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:25.070
and make modifications
at your own pace.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.130
And the Firebase
open-source community

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.150
has continued to
grow over the years.

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.809
And more and more SDKs
from different platform

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.620
had been open source.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:38.530
And now you can help contribute
back to the community.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.429
You can be part of it.

00:08:40.429 --> 00:08:44.350
First, you can help port the
SDK to a different platform

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.970
that we don't support.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.750
Or you can help
making contribution

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:53.520
to the existing SDK by adding
new features or fixing issues.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.920
Now I'm sure you all
got excited about using

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.240
Firebase in your games.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.790
I'm going to pass that to
Patrick to talk about it.

00:09:03.192 --> 00:09:06.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:07.420
PATRICK MARTIN: Hi, everybody.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.640
Thanks, Chen, for
that introduction.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.840
And what I want
to talk to you now

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.450
is how to build-- what it's like
to build a game in Firebase.

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.260
And I think what
will make this easier

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.960
for everyone to understand
is if I first gave you

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.150
guys a little bit of background
from where I'm coming from.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:27.850
So for the past
couple of years, I've

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:31.450
been doing a lot of research
and development and prototyping.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:34.580
And it was the
kind of thing where

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.670
I'd have a few
days, maybe a week,

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.220
to throw a whole bunch
of stuff together, get

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:41.730
a concept off the ground,
and get it testable.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:46.120
And I didn't need that test
to live past the meeting where

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:47.960
it got approved or something.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.410
So I used a very different
stack than I did back

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:55.150
when I worked on asynchronous
multiplayer games.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.640
If you ever work on
something like that,

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:02.030
you need your servers to be
accessible all the time, 24/7,

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:03.730
and for any play length.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:06.690
And the tools to achieve
each one of these

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.040
are typically completely
different sets.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.370
You'll normally rip everything
up from your prototyping

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:15.490
and do something brand
new in production.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:21.200
And I would say
that, yes, there go!

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.520
The job of every developer,
every game developer,

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.560
is really to find the fun.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:30.530
Whether you're in preproduction,
post-production, or working

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:33.050
on a game, your users
aren't paying you

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:35.990
for just pretty
graphics or cool tech.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.210
It's something fun
that they can enjoy.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:41.420
And Firebase, I think,
helps shorten the distance

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:44.870
from any idea you have in your
head to being able to actually

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:47.580
experiment and iterate on it.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:52.010
And not only is this then backed
by Google's Cloud Platform,

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.520
as Chen mentioned, but
if for whatever reason

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.460
Firebase doesn't meet your
needs once you scale up

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:00.710
from prototyping
to production, you

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.860
do have the full brute force
of Google's Cloud behind you.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:07.580
And you can work
directly there if that's

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:08.990
where your needs take you.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:12.110
So I want to show you what it's
like building a game really

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:14.930
quickly using actually a
single player game called

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:17.990
MechaHamster, which is our--

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:22.610
it's our testing project for
Firebase's Unity integration.

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.930
So MechaHamster
is really simple.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:28.100
It's like one of those old,
wooden marble maze games.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:31.070
You just tilt your phone
and get Hammy the hamster

00:11:31.070 --> 00:11:34.350
to the end of this maze up here.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:34.850
Yeah.

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:36.000
Pretty simple.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.940
I'm sure you guys all get it.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:43.820
And what I'm going to do is
enhance this single player game

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:49.130
by giving it a Cloud
backend with secure

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:52.340
and shared data in
it that I probably

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:56.000
wouldn't want to risk otherwise
if I didn't have something

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:59.050
like Firebase to host it on.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:03.510
So the way we decided to
start expanding this game

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:07.440
was to build our
own level editor.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.570
The basic idea behind it
is that normally a designer

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.520
would have to go through
the entire dev stack

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.690
to get a build onto
a phone to test it,

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.440
which could sometimes
take minutes.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.660
And then other people
wouldn't necessarily

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.450
get to play unless you
walked over to their desk

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:26.460
or you pulled it down
from a build server.

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:28.560
So we really wanted
to build something

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:33.480
that it could work on the phone,
press play, test it instantly,

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.640
and just share it
out to the whole team

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:39.480
really quickly, like at
the press of a button.

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:42.460
And since these levels
are just like a 2D grid,

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:45.090
it's a very easy thing
to serialize, store,

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:48.110
and interact with.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:52.120
So we decided to use
Firebase's real-time database.

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:55.300
This represents all of
the data in your database.

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:59.510
It is effectively, a
very large JSON document,

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:03.250
which I know some people love
JSON, some people hate it.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.110
The point isn't that
you're writing JSON

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:09.400
but that many languages and
game engines, like Unity,

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:15.100
can serialize to and from it
just as a library function.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:18.610
So we just hit a node
with the JSON serializer,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:21.640
get all the data
out into a database.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:23.680
And I want to focus
on the real-time bit

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.320
just a little bit.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:28.600
This isn't real-time
like 30 frames

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:30.280
a second, like a video game.

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:32.620
But this is real-time enough
that you could do something

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:37.710
like Google Docs,
collaborative editing.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:40.570
I find that sometimes I can
even type on one computer

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:42.190
and look at another
and kind of know

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:43.960
what I'm doing in a Google Doc.

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.030
You could do the same
sort of synchronization

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.300
in a level editor if you
built out the UI for it.

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.180
But the other really cool
thing a real-time database does

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:55.160
for you is cache everything.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.330
So if you're offline, if you'd
like drove through a tunnel

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:00.250
while you're working
on a level, all

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.580
your writes are still
instant to the database.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.720
So you can use the database
provided in our SDK.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:08.470
It's kind of your
single source of truth.

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:10.870
And then whenever
you go back online,

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:13.280
your local copy doesn't
get stomped with the web.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:16.330
And the web copy doesn't get
stomped with your local copy.

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:19.690
We just replay the transactions
you made while you're offline,

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:23.035
which is kind of a nice way
to protect against data loss

00:14:23.035 --> 00:14:24.570
as you're doing
these migrations.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:28.030
It's kind of a hard
problem if you've ever had

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.740
to implement this on your own.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.500
And a cool thing that
came out of all this work

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:36.870
to build a level editor is a--

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:38.910
well, we're all game
developers, right?

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:40.670
We're at the Game
Developer Conference.

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:43.610
And I'm sure we all had
that moment when we realized

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:46.670
that games are not
magical plastic disks that

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:51.830
appeared in like a game store
or, in my case, cartridges.

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:53.960
For me, the moment
that I realized this

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:56.150
was when I found the
Quake 1 level editor.

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:58.880
And I started making,
actually, really

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:02.300
bad maps where only I knew
the cool weapon drops or like

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:05.270
sniping positions or something.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:09.590
So why couldn't we enable
fans of MechaHamster

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:11.375
to express their
creativity like that

00:15:11.375 --> 00:15:15.950
and maybe join us in
the creative process?

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:19.190
And even though we're storing
this data in the Cloud,

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:23.570
we're able to confidently
enable this kind of curated user

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:27.290
sharing with this
rules language that we

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.270
have with Realtime Database.

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:32.690
This is just a JSON object
with annotations that

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:36.160
lay parallel to your database.

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:37.630
And they're relatively simple.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:40.100
I I've actually
simplified this a bit

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.060
more just so it
fits on the slide.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:44.180
But here we just saying
the node map list

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.910
is readable by the
entire internet.

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:51.350
But by changing this to the
string off not equals to null,

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:54.770
we guarantee that any user
trying to read this map list

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:57.770
will only get it if
they are logged in.

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:00.500
And these statements
you put here

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.270
can be JavaScript expressions.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:05.930
So you can enforce rules like,
only the creator of a map

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:08.590
can use it until they market
it as shared, stuff like that.

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:14.850
And where that auth
came from is actually

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:16.710
from Firebase authentication.

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:21.030
In MechaHamster, we opted to
use email address and password

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:22.950
account management
as well as something

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:25.500
called anonymous sign on.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.660
Anonymous sign on in
particular might sound weird.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:33.090
But all it's doing is
associating your device

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:36.210
with the account instead of
like a username and password.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.790
And later on, if the user
wants to share their data

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:41.580
between devices,
they can migrate up

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.590
to an email address account.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.620
And you can, of course, opt out
of signing in if you just don't

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.270
want these online features.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:52.850
And I should stress that
this email address password

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:57.190
authentication isn't me
being silly and thinking

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:01.750
I can implement my own sign
in logic with all the security

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.970
issues associated with this.

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:08.020
This is a Firebase product and
has all of Google's security

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:09.190
guarantees.

00:17:09.190 --> 00:17:13.369
But we also support all these
other authentication providers.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.760
Now if you're prototyping,
and you're just

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.470
trying to get an idea of the
ground as fast as you can,

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:20.569
use email address and password.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:25.030
But all you have to do is
integrate the client SDKs

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.618
for each one of
these auth providers.

00:17:26.618 --> 00:17:28.410
We do everything else
in the cloud for you,

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.540
validating the user token.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.820
We even let you merge
and migrate accounts.

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:35.880
So GameCenter on iOS
is a new integration.

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:39.810
If you added it to your app,
you could allow your users

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:41.640
to add that to their account.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.590
And all of a sudden,
they have a new way

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:46.478
of signing on that doesn't
require them to start again

00:17:46.478 --> 00:17:47.020
from scratch.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.610
So another thing we wanted
to do is add leaderboards

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:53.390
to MechaHamster.

00:17:53.390 --> 00:17:54.950
This is super-common in games.

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:58.430
And it's so common that we
just made it open-source.

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:01.430
So it can go up here,
grab the source code

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.550
for a sample
leaderboard integration,

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:09.020
actually start playing with
the Realtime Database right

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.140
now in your existing game
writing just a minimal amount

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:15.080
of code, get your feet
wet, decide whether or not

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:16.740
it's right for you.

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:20.360
So that's at Firebase Extended
slash Unity solutions.

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:25.670
So we wanted to augment
our leaderboard though

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:27.170
with user replays.

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:30.560
Now for reasons that might
become apparent later

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:34.880
in my talk, we had replay
recording in MechaHamster.

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:39.776
So we decided we'd
show how people got--

00:18:39.776 --> 00:18:40.540
oh, there we go.

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:42.110
Yeah.

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:46.100
We wanted to show how people got
the high scores that they had.

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:48.620
And you might just
think, hey, I'm

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:51.350
going to use JSON
serializer and Unity

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:53.030
and just dump whatever
data structure

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:57.050
I have for the replays
into the Realtime Database.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:18:59.960
But it turns out even
compressed in a flat buffer,

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.220
these replays are around
30 to 60 kilobytes.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.400
And at some point, the
RealTime is not real time

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.390
just because you're
passing a lot of data.

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:12.560
And also you don't want to
collaboratively edit replays.

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:16.010
To me, that sounds more like
cheating than a feature.

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:18.100
So we needed somewhere
large [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:20.740
to store this replay data.

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:24.040
And we decided to use
Cloud Storage for Firebase.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:25.730
Now this is another database.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:29.170
It's a key value storage where
the key is just any path.

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:32.170
So logically order
this however you want.

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:33.910
And the value is
really anything you

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:35.890
want to put up
there, videos, player

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:38.710
avatars, in our case, replays.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:41.950
And the flow for
uploading a high score now

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:45.520
becomes you add the high
score to the high score table.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:48.580
There's an extra node there
for-- the replay will live.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.450
You start asynchronously
uploading the replay data,

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:53.170
get a callback when
that completes,

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:55.295
and you can just update
the note in the leaderboard

00:19:55.295 --> 00:19:56.900
to point to your replay.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:59.920
There is one problem
with this, though.

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:02.260
Now that we have
two databases, when

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:04.330
a high score is no
longer a high score,

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:07.670
the replay data will leak.

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:12.910
So we use something called
Cloud Functions to listen

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:15.460
for when there's a change,
in this case, a Create

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:17.350
event in the Realtime Database.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:19.840
When that happens, we check
how many high scores there

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.600
are for the map you're
on, knock off the lowest

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:26.350
if there are more than five, and
cleanup the associated replay

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.100
data.

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:32.240
So Cloud Functions are
actually a really awesome tool

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:36.350
for someone like me who's lived
mostly in the client space.

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:39.050
They're just now out
of beta for Firebase.

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:41.510
And they let you run
mobile back end code

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:43.260
without managing the servers.

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:47.180
Again, if I'm prototyping,
this is an awesome shortcut

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:48.600
to get to where we need to be.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.230
And it is still on Google's
Cloud infrastructure.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:55.850
So these are reliable
and scalable.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:58.420
You write these in
JavaScript or TypeScript.

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:02.750
And you have the full MPM
package manager to deal with.

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:05.170
So you can really go crazy here.

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:07.790
They are still secure, so
Firebase authentication still

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:09.000
applies.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.270
Auth not equals to
null is a valid way

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:12.710
to check if a user's log in.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:16.250
And the user is guaranteed
to not be spoofing their user

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:18.440
token or something by now.

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:21.020
And these let you
not only communicate

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:23.330
between different
Firebase products

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.370
but also between
Firebase and the web.

00:21:25.370 --> 00:21:28.010
So just some examples of
what you might use these

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:32.090
for in games, if you have like
asynchronous multiplayer board

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:35.330
games, rather than just
trusting players to not

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:37.250
claim that they roll
nat 20s all the time,

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:40.220
they're the Gandalf of
monopoly or something,

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:43.780
you can push your die rolls
off into a Cloud Function.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:46.130
If you have a gacha
mechanic in your game,

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:48.170
you can use the Cloud
Function to make sure

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:49.753
they have enough
capsules or whatever,

00:21:49.753 --> 00:21:51.260
subtract one, roll the loop.

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:55.820
But if you're using something
like Crashlytics from Firebase,

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:59.450
you can actually listen for a
new crash to happen in the wild

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:02.940
and actually automatically
file a Jira for your team.

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.630
Maybe if a crash
spikes an occurrence,

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:09.590
you send a message to the SOS
channel on your team's Slack,

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:13.910
and everyone starts
rallying around it.

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:17.380
So now it's time to ship a game.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:21.040
And the thing that keeps me up
at night, whenever I hit go,

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.320
is when the inevitable
review comes

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:26.080
in that says, game is a
blank screen on launch

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:27.850
or it crashes on launch.

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:31.100
It worked in all our
devices in the QA test lab.

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:33.250
But there's just something
in the wild we kind

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:35.380
of take account of.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:39.730
So you really need to run
your game on actual hardware.

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.940
You need to test various OS
and hardware combinations

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:45.820
because even if you're using
test-driven development,

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:50.530
something like you're using
a feature in a shader that's

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:54.250
not supported will still cause
a shader compilation error.

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:58.030
Actually once had a game
as blank screen on launch

00:22:58.030 --> 00:23:01.930
because the OpenGL context
claimed that it supported

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.120
a level of antialiasing
that it actually didn't.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:07.090
And it just failed to
create my context for me.

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:08.890
And you really want
these to be automatic,

00:23:08.890 --> 00:23:11.050
like not just a
human going through.

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:13.720
You want to be able to
shove these up to a server

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:17.860
where some cadence built into
like your build pipeline and be

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:23.780
able to know the very
moment a build fails.

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:27.730
So for MechaHamster,
we're using Robo tests

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:29.250
in Firebase Test Lab.

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:32.180
Now MechaHamster didn't
have many unit tests.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:34.270
And at least, in my
experience in the industry,

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:38.500
that is really common
in video games.

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:41.950
But with Robo tests, we can
just upload the production APK

00:23:41.950 --> 00:23:43.840
and-- well, the way
I like to imagine

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:45.820
it is a friendly
little robot walks

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:49.670
into your game, kind of
stares at your view hierarchy,

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:52.060
and then presses all the
buttons as fast as it possibly

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:55.740
can until it crashes
or it times out.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.220
And this will run
on real hardware.

00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:01.180
There's literally like
racks of phones or something

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:04.393
in a data center somewhere.

00:24:04.393 --> 00:24:05.810
And what's really
cool about this,

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:08.500
especially for the
kind of test it does,

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.150
it might be hard
for me to reproduce.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.100
I might C&amp;R the bug
just because like it

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:14.650
required tapping in a specific
spot in the splash screen

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:15.850
or something.

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.050
So it records the
video, shows you

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:21.880
little circles of where
this robot was interacting.

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.310
You get the stack
trace if it crashed.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.880
You get the log of everything
that happened leading up to it.

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:30.790
And you know that this ran on
kind of a clean environment.

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:33.010
There's not like
that weird app that

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:35.650
kills applications in the
background to theoretically

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:37.510
save battery life or something.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:41.540
This is just a stock device
that you're running this on.

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:44.150
But some engineers
in the room might

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:47.450
have noticed that I said this
robot walks around and looks

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:49.560
at your view hierarchy.

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:51.140
And we're game developers.

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:56.310
Our view hierarchy is
probably just a GL Context.

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:59.510
So this seems kind of useless.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:03.200
So, of course, we decided
to use our AI Chops

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:07.310
to create something that we
call AI assisted monkey testing.

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:11.000
This uses machine vision to
look at the output of your game

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:14.840
like a human, find things that
look interactable, usually

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:18.960
buttons, and concentrate the
random presses at these buttons

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:23.540
so you don't have to expose
your custom UI to Robo test

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:25.760
or, in this case, AI
system monkey testing

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.470
to get a useful test.

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:31.280
As you see here the AI
assisted monkey testing video

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:34.440
is already to the logical
end of this unit test,

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:36.680
whereas the Robo test
is still clicking around

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:39.180
in some corner of
the screen somewhere.

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:43.040
If anything else, this is just a
safety net for your existing QA

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:43.540
team.

00:25:43.540 --> 00:25:47.030
You don't have to do any
additional integration or APK.

00:25:47.030 --> 00:25:49.190
This is running
on actual hardware

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:50.870
in a data center,
as I pointed out,

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:53.900
or on virtual devices if you
want to test like weird OS

00:25:53.900 --> 00:25:55.940
or screen combinations.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.440
And, yeah!

00:25:57.440 --> 00:25:58.320
It's pretty cool.

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:00.620
Had another bullet
point, but, yeah.

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:01.900
I already went over it.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:05.210
So there's another
kind of unit testing

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:09.980
though that we usually call it
like demo loops or a profiling

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:10.730
test.

00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:14.630
In Firebase Test Lab,
we call it game loops.

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:16.812
And the general idea
of this is that you

00:26:16.812 --> 00:26:19.400
know what parts of the
game you need to hammer,

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:21.320
how to implode all
the shaders, get

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:23.390
a bunch of geometry on screen.

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:26.180
And not only do you want to
see if phones will run it

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:27.860
without crashing,
but you might also

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:30.277
want to profile this and make
sure you're hitting your min

00:26:30.277 --> 00:26:32.270
frame rate targets
on all your devices

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:36.470
and kind of build up a list of
which devices you need a lower

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:40.230
performance profile
on or something.

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:43.610
So once again, you upload your
production APK to the cloud,

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:46.730
run it on real devices that
span the range of low end

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:49.700
to like flagship phones.

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:53.420
And the way this works in a
production environment is it

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:55.310
sends something called
an intent, which,

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:57.620
if you're not familiar
with native Android Dev,

00:26:57.620 --> 00:26:59.690
you're just coming from
the Unity background,

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:04.260
this is basically a command line
argument but to an Android app.

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:07.590
And I've spent a lot
of time in Unity.

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:09.465
I wasn't quite sure
how I'd get this in.

00:27:09.465 --> 00:27:10.840
So when I looked
at MechaHamster,

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:16.170
I found this Game
Loop manager class.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:17.645
I'm not sure where it came from.

00:27:17.645 --> 00:27:19.020
It's actually from
this code lab.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.600
It's very easy to follow.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:25.600
If you already profile
or use demo loops,

00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:27.150
I'd recommend--
walk through this.

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:30.060
And you just instantly
grow your QA test pool

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.322
for this sort of thing.

00:27:31.322 --> 00:27:33.030
Again, even if you
don't want to do this,

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:34.988
if you want to put the
minimum amount of effort

00:27:34.988 --> 00:27:37.120
possible into testing--

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.120
I'm not saying that's a
bad thing, but sometimes

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:40.590
it's the reality--

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:42.360
just upload your APK now.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:47.242
And you just gave your QA an
extra little bit of safety net

00:27:47.242 --> 00:27:49.200
as they go through the
normal approval process.

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:56.850
So you're still going to
have crashes in the field.

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:58.350
You can't catch everything.

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:00.180
Again, there's like
that crazy app that

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:03.870
kills games in the background
and people root their phones

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:05.250
and all this other stuff.

00:28:05.250 --> 00:28:08.750
You're still going to
need crash reporting.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:13.280
And Crashlytics is kind
of a really neat way

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:16.370
to gather crashes from
the field and sort them

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:19.760
by frequency, device type,
OS version, everything

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:20.630
you need to--

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:22.010
stack traces as
well-- everything

00:28:22.010 --> 00:28:25.760
you need to not
only figure out why

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:28.970
a crash happened but see if it's
something you need to address.

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:32.510
Because the reality is
that some phones will just

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:35.240
have a marginal market share.

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:40.280
And if he cannot reliably fix
the crash in a short amount

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:43.250
of time, if this phone is
a fraction of a percent

00:28:43.250 --> 00:28:48.320
of your market, it may be more
fiscally pertinent to just

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:50.180
blacklist it on the Play store.

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:53.000
But if when the brand new
flagship phone comes out,

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:56.480
and your game is crashing
all the time on it--

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:59.060
this actually happened to
me when the first multicore

00:28:59.060 --> 00:29:00.860
phones came out--

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:05.150
if you have that Cloud
Functions hook running,

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:07.640
you might get a spike
in the SOS channel,

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.400
this bug is happening
a whole lot.

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:10.950
And you can rally
your team around,

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:12.408
make sure you get
that preorder in,

00:29:12.408 --> 00:29:17.090
and start testing on the new
phone if that happens to you.

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:23.000
And Crashlytics is now
integrated with Unity,

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:24.650
which is really awesome to me.

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:27.355
Again, in the past, what would
happen is I'd get like the C++

00:29:27.355 --> 00:29:31.160
stack trays, and I'd have to
bring that into the IL2CPP

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:34.550
output, and then try to figure
out what C# generated that,

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:36.217
which was kind of a process.

00:29:36.217 --> 00:29:37.550
There are helpful guides for it.

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:39.930
But it took me a
little bit of time.

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:43.190
In this case, I
accidentally cleared a field

00:29:43.190 --> 00:29:45.440
in a game object.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.000
And I got a stack trace.

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:49.400
And Crashlytics,
almost instantly,

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:52.070
I was refreshing
the dashboard that

00:29:52.070 --> 00:29:54.810
showed me exactly where
to look to find this.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:56.450
And in this case,
this isn't even a bug

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:58.100
I would have made
it as a programmer.

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:00.320
Or I could have made
it as a programmer,

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:03.380
but also a designer or an
artist might have made this.

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:07.310
Or it could have just
happened from version control.

00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:11.050
So Crashlytics, is pretty
much a free integration

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.360
if you're already
using Firebase.

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:17.440
So you might as well
just drop it in, again,

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:18.940
for a little bit
of extra safety net

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:22.412
just to bump the quality of
your app up a little higher.

00:30:22.412 --> 00:30:24.370
With that, I'm going to
hand this over to Sumit

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:27.182
to talk to you about growth.

00:30:27.182 --> 00:30:30.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:32.457 --> 00:30:33.790
SUMIT CHANDEL: All right, y'all.

00:30:33.790 --> 00:30:34.810
So we built the game.

00:30:34.810 --> 00:30:35.830
We shipped the game.

00:30:35.830 --> 00:30:37.820
And now it's time
to grow the game.

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:39.670
Now when you grow
your game, there's

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:42.003
the traditional marketing
methods and teams

00:30:42.003 --> 00:30:43.420
that your company
or startup might

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:45.610
be using where
they run a campaign

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:47.703
and try to grow your
player or user base.

00:30:47.703 --> 00:30:49.870
And there's a lot of different
tools and innovations

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:51.620
available to those teams there.

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:54.268
And that's probably something
you want to continue to use.

00:30:54.268 --> 00:30:55.810
That, however, is
different than what

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:57.850
I wanted to talk about
here in the context

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:01.120
of Firebase developer
services and Google Cloud.

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:05.020
So within the developer context,
what you want to be able to do

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:07.783
is easily A/B test new features.

00:31:07.783 --> 00:31:08.950
So you've shipped your game.

00:31:08.950 --> 00:31:10.340
You know where you're at now.

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:13.030
And you want to figure out
as you release new features,

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:14.013
are they good or bad?

00:31:14.013 --> 00:31:15.430
How are they going
to be received?

00:31:15.430 --> 00:31:17.055
And certainly, you
don't want to damage

00:31:17.055 --> 00:31:18.700
what you've already released.

00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:21.760
So you want to be able to easily
A/B test and then furthermore,

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:23.620
measure the results
of those A/B tests

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:26.650
as well to determine
their effectiveness.

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:28.180
Personally, I'm
still A/B testing

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:30.280
whether Gregory's
Coffee, Starbucks Coffee,

00:31:30.280 --> 00:31:33.520
or Third Rail Coffee makes me
more productive in the morning.

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:37.820
Results so far, inconclusive.

00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:38.960
All right.

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:42.230
So I'll set the stage with this
question or this opening use

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:45.780
case of, how can I track
how my game is doing now?

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:47.990
And how can it easily
test new features

00:31:47.990 --> 00:31:49.970
and see if those are good?

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:52.405
So we'll look at the first
part of this question first,

00:31:52.405 --> 00:31:54.530
which is, how can I track
how my game is doing now?

00:31:57.350 --> 00:31:59.100
OK, so to track how
your game is doing,

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.080
you've got to look at data.

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:03.650
And depending on the type
of game that you have

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:05.570
and the number of
players, we could

00:32:05.570 --> 00:32:07.950
be talking about a lot of data.

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:10.100
So take, for example,
a massively multiplayer

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:12.950
online RBG game
where you're not just

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:15.590
logging single events of
users tapping on your screen

00:32:15.590 --> 00:32:18.410
or interacting with your game as
single events that are getting

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:21.590
recorded, but you're
collecting a stream of events

00:32:21.590 --> 00:32:25.130
over a long interval of
time, maybe a few hours.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:27.498
In that kind of game, a
player might be interacting

00:32:27.498 --> 00:32:28.790
with other players in the game.

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:30.320
And you're logging that.

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:32.570
They might be buying,
selling, or trading items.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:34.200
And you're logging those.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:36.980
They might be completing quests
or accepting new missions

00:32:36.980 --> 00:32:39.170
or taking a certain
direction on your map.

00:32:39.170 --> 00:32:42.260
And these are all events
that you're collecting

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:44.480
over hours for one player.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:46.700
Now imagine if you've
got thousands or millions

00:32:46.700 --> 00:32:47.720
of players.

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:50.400
That's a lot of data
that you're collecting.

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:52.220
So hence, the image
of this data center

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:54.870
to represent a
whole lot of data,

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:57.500
for example, on one of Google
services that relies on this.

00:33:00.670 --> 00:33:03.762
So that's where Google
Cloud BigQuery can come in.

00:33:03.762 --> 00:33:05.220
So if you're a
large game publisher

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:08.700
and you've already collected
a lot of player data,

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:13.350
Google Cloud BigQuery has
a free bulk loading tool

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:16.530
where you can upload all
of that data into BigQuery

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:18.300
and then start
slicing and dicing it

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:23.160
using SQL-like queries with the
computational power behind them

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:24.960
to actually return the results.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:28.140
So you can start establishing
a view of what your players are

00:33:28.140 --> 00:33:30.570
doing in your game.

00:33:30.570 --> 00:33:34.290
A couple of quick things
to note about BigQuery

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:36.230
is that, one, it's
fully managed and server

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:40.500
and, second, storage and compute
are independent from each other

00:33:40.500 --> 00:33:43.800
and can scale up or
scale down and on demand.

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:46.590
So that kind of flexibility can
save a lot of business costs

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:49.620
from your side because you
don't have to invest upfront

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:51.900
in building your own
infrastructure for logging

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.698
or storage pipelines to
manage all this data.

00:33:54.698 --> 00:33:56.490
And, of course, storing
that amount of data

00:33:56.490 --> 00:33:58.330
is already a pain let
alone analyzing it

00:33:58.330 --> 00:33:59.580
to figure out what's going on.

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:05.020
But maybe you're
not ready to invest

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:06.850
a lot of logging
infrastructure in the game

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:08.230
as you just released it.

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:09.940
Maybe you just want
to release your game

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:11.607
and start getting
some kind of analytics

00:34:11.607 --> 00:34:14.250
without having to invest in
that logging or storage pipeline

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:15.983
infrastructure.

00:34:15.983 --> 00:34:17.900
So for such a situation,
you can use something

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:19.550
like Google Analytics
for Firebase.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:25.409
The way it works is pretty much
as soon as you add the Firebase

00:34:25.409 --> 00:34:27.449
SDK to your project,
you're going

00:34:27.449 --> 00:34:31.357
to start getting out of the box
events automatically reported.

00:34:31.357 --> 00:34:33.190
And when you log into
your Firebase console,

00:34:33.190 --> 00:34:35.398
you're going to see these
reports automatically being

00:34:35.398 --> 00:34:37.139
generated for your game.

00:34:37.139 --> 00:34:40.710
So that includes things like
in-app purchase revenue, user

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:44.340
engagement, retention, daily
active users, and so forth.

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:49.400
But more importantly,
Analytics also

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:51.949
allows you to use custom events
that you can log in your game

00:34:51.949 --> 00:34:52.773
as well.

00:34:52.773 --> 00:34:55.190
So those are things that are
more specific and interesting

00:34:55.190 --> 00:34:56.750
for your game.

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:58.790
For MechaHamster, this
might be things like,

00:34:58.790 --> 00:35:00.170
did anyone use the map editor?

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:01.670
Did someone create a map?

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:04.550
How many times did they retry
a level before they gave up?

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:07.070
Did they even manage
to finish the level?

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:08.450
Which levels did they select?

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:09.800
These kind of things
are custom events

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:11.565
that you might want to
record or that we'd want

00:35:11.565 --> 00:35:12.700
to record for MechaHamster.

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:15.500
And you can also choose
that for your own games

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:17.990
and have it available in
the Analytics dashboard.

00:35:20.107 --> 00:35:22.440
And then while we're talking
about creating these custom

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:25.890
events, you'll want to mark some
of these as conversion events

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:26.980
as well.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:29.370
And you can do so in
the Firebase console

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:31.110
by hitting those toggles--

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:34.980
one second, I'm trying to
click the thing, OK, sorry--

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:39.130
by hitting those toggles to
mark whatever events that you're

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:41.290
recording as conversion events.

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:42.850
So those conversion
events are things

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:44.800
that are especially
important for your app

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:46.953
and what you want to
direct your users to do.

00:35:46.953 --> 00:35:49.370
So these could be things like,
did they create an account?

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:52.640
Did they invite your friend
to the game or their friend

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:53.350
to the game?

00:35:53.350 --> 00:35:56.430
Did they give premium
currency to their games

00:35:56.430 --> 00:35:58.865
or give something to their
friends for the game,

00:35:58.865 --> 00:35:59.740
those kind of things.

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:03.080
Sorry.

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:04.247
I need go back to one slide.

00:36:06.890 --> 00:36:09.550
And the other advantage
with marking these things

00:36:09.550 --> 00:36:12.050
as conversion events, these
important things for your game,

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:13.880
is that Firebase
can use that to feed

00:36:13.880 --> 00:36:18.087
that back to any ad campaigns
you have to attract more users.

00:36:18.087 --> 00:36:19.670
And it'll tailor the
audiences that it

00:36:19.670 --> 00:36:22.377
goes after there based
on the conversion data

00:36:22.377 --> 00:36:23.210
that you care about.

00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:29.990
So with Google, with
Google Cloud BigQuery,

00:36:29.990 --> 00:36:31.640
and with Firebase
Analytics, you're

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:33.775
starting to get a picture.

00:36:33.775 --> 00:36:36.150
You're starting to be able to
paint a picture, basically,

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:38.660
of what is going on in your
game and what your players are

00:36:38.660 --> 00:36:39.860
doing.

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:42.110
So we answered the first
part of this question of,

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:44.285
how can I track how
my game is doing now?

00:36:44.285 --> 00:36:46.910
And once you know that, you want
to look at the second part of,

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:49.577
now how can I easily
test new features

00:36:49.577 --> 00:36:50.660
and see if those are good?

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:56.150
So typically, when you want
to test a new set of features,

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:58.445
you might have an
alpha or beta channel

00:36:58.445 --> 00:37:00.320
where you'll release
that new set of features

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:03.550
and expose it to a test group.

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:07.420
And that's well and good but can
often have a lot of overhead.

00:37:07.420 --> 00:37:10.390
For one, you have to match the
cadence of your team's release

00:37:10.390 --> 00:37:14.020
cycle to get your build
available for each

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:14.800
of these channels.

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:17.050
And then, of course, you
need different build variants

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:19.210
that you deploy to
alpha or beta, which can

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:21.690
start becoming a bit of a pain.

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:24.030
Furthermore, if you're
releasing a new feature

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:26.940
and you want to easily get
feedback on it, if you're just

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:28.720
releasing it to
alpha or beta users,

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:30.510
you might not be
getting a large amount

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:32.820
of data versus
your whole audience

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:34.895
or at least a larger
percentage of your audience

00:37:34.895 --> 00:37:36.270
where you're
getting the feedback

00:37:36.270 --> 00:37:38.850
behind that new feature and
being able to determine--

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:40.520
and validate that it's working.

00:37:43.605 --> 00:37:44.980
And then here's
another use case.

00:37:44.980 --> 00:37:48.820
What if you want to just
release a temporary, seasonal,

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:51.102
or promotional new
feature in your game?

00:37:51.102 --> 00:37:52.810
So in the case of
MechaHamster, let's say

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:55.090
we want to change the
color of the hamster ball

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:57.502
to white and red
just for Canada Day

00:37:57.502 --> 00:37:58.960
when it's Canada
Day in that user's

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:02.240
device in the date/time
of that user's device

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:03.400
and for Canadian users.

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:09.990
Oh, sorry, I have
here one slide back.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:12.910
So including that
one change, one

00:38:12.910 --> 00:38:15.300
seasonal change or temporary
change into your build

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.300
can complicate that build
because you've already

00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:18.410
got a few things planned.

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:20.118
And if you're using
this one new feature,

00:38:20.118 --> 00:38:22.360
it can add some delay to that.

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:26.110
In addition to that, if
there's other higher priority

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:28.780
issues that come up for
your team's current build,

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:29.918
this might get dropped.

00:38:29.918 --> 00:38:32.210
There might be other things
that they have to focus on.

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:34.120
And this is not really
something they can do.

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:37.620
And then you risk
losing it entirely.

00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:39.230
So for both of those
use cases, when

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:41.540
you want to release
new features and easily

00:38:41.540 --> 00:38:44.150
test them as well as for
seasonal changes you want

00:38:44.150 --> 00:38:46.280
to introduce in
your game, there's

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:49.130
Firebase Remote Config
that we want to introduce.

00:38:49.130 --> 00:38:51.020
So if you haven't
heard of this before,

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:52.910
you can think of it
as a key value pair

00:38:52.910 --> 00:38:56.450
that-- a key value store
that lives in the Cloud.

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:58.820
And it allows you
to control the look

00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:01.700
and feel of your game or
the behavior of your game

00:39:01.700 --> 00:39:04.100
remotely without
having to rebuild

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:06.440
different versions of
your app and deploy it

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:08.315
to all the different
channels and app stores.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:13.160
So this is the way it works.

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:16.722
Going back to our example for
the Canada Day hamster ball,

00:39:16.722 --> 00:39:18.680
here's some default values
for what the hamster

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:20.090
ball looks like now.

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:23.060
So we've got ball_color,
ball_glow, and ball_size

00:39:23.060 --> 00:39:26.810
as Remote Config parameters we
defined with these defaults.

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:29.390
So whenever the user starts up
the phone for the first time,

00:39:29.390 --> 00:39:30.440
these are normally the
values that they'll

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:31.232
see for their ball.

00:39:34.300 --> 00:39:37.000
Next, we introduce
some new values

00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:38.950
for the ball, ball
color being white

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:40.920
and the ball glow being red.

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:44.050
And we can condition those
or gate them on specific--

00:39:44.050 --> 00:39:45.430
any kind of conditions.

00:39:45.430 --> 00:39:47.050
In this case, it
would be that it

00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:48.940
is Canada Day on
the user's phone

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:50.930
on their date/time
on their device

00:39:50.930 --> 00:39:52.510
and that they're
located in Canada.

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:55.720
For those users, we want
to push these values.

00:39:55.720 --> 00:39:57.430
These will then be
waiting in the Cloud,

00:39:57.430 --> 00:40:02.374
ready to replace the values
that we've set as the default.

00:40:02.374 --> 00:40:05.450
And then when the condition
hits and everything is true,

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:07.640
it'll combine those
values, pulling the delta,

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:11.670
and then change the look of the
ball for those Canadian users.

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:14.070
Another cool thing about
the way Remote Config works

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:17.580
is that it only downloads
the delta, or the difference,

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:20.010
the diff of the values for
the Remote Config variables

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:21.660
and not the whole
set every time.

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:24.060
So you're not going to eat
up a lot of your user's data

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:25.140
by using Remote Config.

00:40:28.102 --> 00:40:29.810
Now you might be
thinking, wait a minute.

00:40:29.810 --> 00:40:31.993
Isn't this kind of like
feature rollout flagging?

00:40:31.993 --> 00:40:32.660
And yeah, it is.

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:34.770
That's kind of the
idea behind it.

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:38.090
So it allows you to easily
control feature rollouts

00:40:38.090 --> 00:40:41.630
for your game without having
to redeploy different builds

00:40:41.630 --> 00:40:45.930
and enable and disable that
via the Firebase console.

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:47.458
One thing I should
mention actually

00:40:47.458 --> 00:40:49.500
in the previous slide--
can I go two slides back?

00:40:49.500 --> 00:40:50.580
Sorry.

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:52.830
So those new values
that you set,

00:40:52.830 --> 00:40:55.893
you'd actually set those in
the Firebase console directly.

00:40:55.893 --> 00:40:57.810
So you can do it through
the Firebase console.

00:40:57.810 --> 00:40:59.980
Or you can do it
via the REST API.

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:01.630
So that's how you
can set and condition

00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:03.047
the new values you
want to set on.

00:41:07.570 --> 00:41:08.070
OK.

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:09.960
So we saw a little
bit of how to use

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.480
Remote Config for this
new seasonal feature

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:14.610
that we want to
release for Canada Day.

00:41:14.610 --> 00:41:17.560
But how about like for
more general use cases?

00:41:17.560 --> 00:41:20.220
There's some other times where
Remote Config can be helpful.

00:41:20.220 --> 00:41:22.470
And more importantly, when
you're testing or releasing

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:25.330
these new features, you want to
get the feedback behind them.

00:41:25.330 --> 00:41:28.510
So let's take a look at that.

00:41:28.510 --> 00:41:29.010
OK.

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:31.100
So taking a look back
at the custom events

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:34.550
that I created for Google
Analytics for Firebase

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:36.920
for MechaHamster, if
you look at the ones

00:41:36.920 --> 00:41:38.680
on the right-hand
side, I'm collecting

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:41.420
retries, whether the
user finished the level,

00:41:41.420 --> 00:41:43.220
and what levels
they've selected.

00:41:43.220 --> 00:41:46.190
Well, you could imagine that,
let's say a user who just

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:49.100
started playing this game, they
have a large number of retries

00:41:49.100 --> 00:41:52.040
for a given level or they never
did manage to complete one

00:41:52.040 --> 00:41:54.890
or they've only selected a
certain group of levels that

00:41:54.890 --> 00:41:56.910
are fairly easy to complete.

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:59.210
You can imagine that those
are beginners to my game.

00:42:01.877 --> 00:42:04.460
Well, one cool thing about the
way Remote Config and Analytics

00:42:04.460 --> 00:42:07.940
works is that it allows you
to create different audiences

00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:10.130
based on these custom
events you can filter or out

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:13.250
of the box events as
well in Analytics.

00:42:13.250 --> 00:42:15.140
In this case, for
MechaHamster, I'm

00:42:15.140 --> 00:42:17.200
creating a beginners audience.

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:20.180
And I want to target Remote
Config variables only

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:21.390
to that audience.

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:24.200
I'm defining that
as any players who

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:27.270
have a number of retries
recorded as greater than 5

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:29.230
and who haven't
completed a level.

00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:30.980
So I've identified
this beginner audience.

00:42:30.980 --> 00:42:33.880
And for them, I'm going to
change the values of my Remote

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:35.720
Config parameters
and set the ball

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:39.152
size to big and the gravity
to 0.5 to make the game easier

00:42:39.152 --> 00:42:39.860
for them to play.

00:42:44.140 --> 00:42:44.640
OK.

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:46.530
So that's cool, right?

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:49.140
I can easily release new things.

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:52.020
But what about different
variants that I want to try?

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:54.300
So for example, for
my beginner audience,

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.880
that new ball size
and gravity that I set

00:42:56.880 --> 00:43:01.970
might make the game way too
easy for them and quite boring.

00:43:01.970 --> 00:43:04.780
For cases where I want to
test multiple variations

00:43:04.780 --> 00:43:07.750
or combinations of these
to see which one works best

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:10.240
for my beginner
audience, there's

00:43:10.240 --> 00:43:12.250
Firebase A/B Testing
as well that works

00:43:12.250 --> 00:43:14.260
in combination
with Remote Config

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:15.960
and Analytics for that purpose.

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:22.370
And here are the steps for
setting up A/B Testing.

00:43:22.370 --> 00:43:25.460
So the first thing is
you define your target.

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:28.207
Whether that's 5% of
your total audience

00:43:28.207 --> 00:43:30.290
or whether that's all your
users who speak Korean,

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:32.810
you can set that up
however you like.

00:43:32.810 --> 00:43:35.810
For MechaHamster, that's going
to be the beginners audience

00:43:35.810 --> 00:43:38.390
that I defined earlier.

00:43:38.390 --> 00:43:40.883
Step two is you
create your variance.

00:43:40.883 --> 00:43:43.550
So again, for MechaHamster, that
would be different combinations

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:45.680
of ball size and
gravity, so just

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:51.030
to see which combination works
best for this beginner group.

00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:54.390
Step three is to determine
the goal for your A/B test.

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:56.250
So these can be out
of the box goals that

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:59.250
come from Analytics, things
like daily user engagement

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:00.300
and that kind of thing.

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:02.687
It can also be custom
events that you care about.

00:44:02.687 --> 00:44:03.520
So what is the goal?

00:44:03.520 --> 00:44:04.230
What are the things
that you want

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:05.897
to measure with these
different variants

00:44:05.897 --> 00:44:08.568
to see if your experiment
is successful or not?

00:44:08.568 --> 00:44:10.110
For MechaHamster,
that would probably

00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:13.785
be that I want my daily
user engagement to increase,

00:44:13.785 --> 00:44:16.260
I want the number of retries
to go down so that they're

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:17.760
having an easier time.

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:20.400
And I might add secondary goals.

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:22.890
For example, user retention
is a secondary goal

00:44:22.890 --> 00:44:25.800
that I'd add here because I want
to make sure I'm not negatively

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:27.720
affecting the
retention of my game

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:30.462
among other beginners in
that group who might find

00:44:30.462 --> 00:44:31.920
all those variants
boring and might

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:33.570
stop playing the game entirely.

00:44:33.570 --> 00:44:36.480
So you can choose what goals you
want for your A/B test as well

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:38.290
and set those here.

00:44:38.290 --> 00:44:40.860
And then the last step is
just to kind of wait and relax

00:44:40.860 --> 00:44:43.500
and let the experiments
run for a couple of weeks

00:44:43.500 --> 00:44:45.982
and start generating some
data that you can look at.

00:44:45.982 --> 00:44:47.940
By the end of that, you'll
be able to determine

00:44:47.940 --> 00:44:50.492
if there's one group that
performed extremely well

00:44:50.492 --> 00:44:52.200
and then roll that
out as the new control

00:44:52.200 --> 00:44:54.510
more broadly among your
beginner audience or whatever

00:44:54.510 --> 00:44:55.593
other audience you define.

00:44:59.530 --> 00:45:03.410
So thus far, we're using Remote
Config to release new features.

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:05.750
We're using A/B testing
to test different variants

00:45:05.750 --> 00:45:07.590
and figure out
which one works best

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:09.480
and then roll that
out more broadly.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:12.065
But this up to now has all
been reactive things we've

00:45:12.065 --> 00:45:13.190
been doing for our players.

00:45:13.190 --> 00:45:16.730
We've been seeing how
they behave so far

00:45:16.730 --> 00:45:19.820
and then basically adding
new features based on what

00:45:19.820 --> 00:45:21.940
we've seen at that point.

00:45:21.940 --> 00:45:23.690
Wouldn't it be cool
if we could anticipate

00:45:23.690 --> 00:45:26.790
what they're going
to do and, in fact,

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:28.880
if we could predict
the future and predict

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:31.980
how our users can behave
instead of reacting to it?

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:35.010
So if only there was someone
who could tell us our futures.

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:36.530
Well, there is.

00:45:36.530 --> 00:45:39.800
And that's Firebase
Predictions, another product

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:42.470
available in Firebase.

00:45:42.470 --> 00:45:44.420
So Firebase can
harness the power

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:46.340
of machine learning
to create groups

00:45:46.340 --> 00:45:50.090
of users who are predicted to
exhibit some kind of behavior

00:45:50.090 --> 00:45:51.650
in your app.

00:45:51.650 --> 00:45:54.770
The general idea is that as your
players are playing your game,

00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:58.190
Google Analytics is collecting a
bunch of data and storing that.

00:45:58.190 --> 00:45:59.690
And then Predictions
can take a look

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:02.600
at that data to see if
there's any patterns that

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:05.870
exist for things that these
players have done before they

00:46:05.870 --> 00:46:07.340
took some other desired action.

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:13.960
So let's say, for example,
that desired action is

00:46:13.960 --> 00:46:15.520
a player who spent
money in your game

00:46:15.520 --> 00:46:17.590
or made an in-app purchase.

00:46:17.590 --> 00:46:20.200
What Predictions will
do is go ahead and look

00:46:20.200 --> 00:46:21.970
at users who have
already made purchases

00:46:21.970 --> 00:46:24.030
and look at the past data.

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:27.140
Then it will use a neural
network powered by TensorFlow

00:46:27.140 --> 00:46:29.930
to see if there are any patterns
that these users have exhibited

00:46:29.930 --> 00:46:31.222
before they made that purchase.

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:37.410
Once it does that,
it will start making

00:46:37.410 --> 00:46:40.380
groups of users categorized
as likely spenders

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:42.210
or unlikely spenders.

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:47.790
And once those groups have
been identified by Predictions,

00:46:47.790 --> 00:46:50.700
you can use those groups in
other features in Firebase.

00:46:50.700 --> 00:46:52.590
And you can target
those predicted

00:46:52.590 --> 00:46:55.360
users who are predicted
to spend in your game.

00:46:55.360 --> 00:46:58.440
So whether that's in Remote
Config or in A/B tests,

00:46:58.440 --> 00:47:00.493
you can now choose that
group and specifically

00:47:00.493 --> 00:47:02.910
target different changes that
you want to roll out to just

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:04.750
them.

00:47:04.750 --> 00:47:07.530
So let's look at a few examples
of different developers

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:10.280
who have done this.

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:13.020
Nope, we're going to look
at that later [LAUGHS]..

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:13.970
Can I go back one?

00:47:13.970 --> 00:47:15.340
Can I go back one slide?

00:47:15.340 --> 00:47:16.780
All right.

00:47:16.780 --> 00:47:20.020
So how do you enable
this magic of machine

00:47:20.020 --> 00:47:21.222
learning to your games?

00:47:21.222 --> 00:47:22.930
Do you have to go back
to school and take

00:47:22.930 --> 00:47:24.950
a neural network [INAUDIBLE]
programming class

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:27.040
and graduate from that again?

00:47:27.040 --> 00:47:27.790
It might not hurt.

00:47:27.790 --> 00:47:29.770
But you don't have
to do any of that.

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:33.590
You can actually just press this
button in the Firebase console.

00:47:33.590 --> 00:47:36.790
So once you press this,
it turns Predictions on.

00:47:36.790 --> 00:47:39.220
And from that point, it's
going to start collecting data.

00:47:39.220 --> 00:47:40.840
And within a few
weeks, you should

00:47:40.840 --> 00:47:42.923
have some Predictions data
that you can play with.

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:47.880
Now out of the box, the
two different events

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:49.860
that Predictions works
for is to determine

00:47:49.860 --> 00:47:52.080
spenders and
nonspenders and also

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:55.570
users who might churn from
your app or continue using it.

00:47:55.570 --> 00:47:58.050
But in addition to those, you
can use other custom events

00:47:58.050 --> 00:47:59.820
that you care about
and use Predictions

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:02.740
to figure out if those actions
are likely to happen as well.

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:05.580
So those are those custom
events that I mentioned earlier

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:07.350
that you would mark
as conversion events

00:48:07.350 --> 00:48:10.093
or things that are particularly
important for your game.

00:48:10.093 --> 00:48:11.010
So you can mark those.

00:48:11.010 --> 00:48:13.468
And then Predictions will start
training over that as well.

00:48:16.640 --> 00:48:17.140
OK.

00:48:17.140 --> 00:48:19.870
So here's where I was getting
to earlier about examples

00:48:19.870 --> 00:48:21.520
of different game
publishers who have

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:24.040
used some combinations
of these features

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:26.000
to realize some
pretty good value.

00:48:26.000 --> 00:48:28.240
So in this case,
there's Halfbrick.

00:48:28.240 --> 00:48:30.340
What they did is that
they use Predictions

00:48:30.340 --> 00:48:33.490
to determine churners,
people who are likely to stop

00:48:33.490 --> 00:48:34.930
playing their game.

00:48:34.930 --> 00:48:37.540
And in that group, they
targeted notifications

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:40.600
that gifted them premium
currency in their game.

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:43.360
And in doing so,
they saw a 20% boost

00:48:43.360 --> 00:48:45.327
in seven-day user retention.

00:48:45.327 --> 00:48:47.410
And it performed better
than a fixed reward system

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:50.420
that they had earlier.

00:48:50.420 --> 00:48:51.770
There's also Rockbite.

00:48:51.770 --> 00:48:56.220
So they used a combination of
predictions and Remote Config.

00:48:56.220 --> 00:48:59.630
And in their case, they use the
groups that check for spenders.

00:48:59.630 --> 00:49:01.550
And for those that were
determined to spend,

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:06.680
they changed the store layout
to better match their patterns.

00:49:06.680 --> 00:49:08.660
And for those who are
determined to not spend,

00:49:08.660 --> 00:49:10.868
they had a different layout
through the Remote Config

00:49:10.868 --> 00:49:11.995
that they'd configured.

00:49:11.995 --> 00:49:14.120
And in doing so, they
increased their profitability

00:49:14.120 --> 00:49:17.420
and remain competitive
in an already competitive

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:19.152
mobile gaming industry.

00:49:22.160 --> 00:49:22.660
OK.

00:49:22.660 --> 00:49:24.280
So at this point,
let's say, you've

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:25.780
used analytics to
collect insights

00:49:25.780 --> 00:49:27.860
into your data for
Google Cloud BigQuery

00:49:27.860 --> 00:49:30.240
and upload your data
to slice and dice it.

00:49:30.240 --> 00:49:33.490
You're using Remote
Config, A/B Testing

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:35.290
to test different variants.

00:49:35.290 --> 00:49:36.250
And this is all great.

00:49:36.250 --> 00:49:39.040
And let's say, as a result of
this, you've got a lot of users

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:40.175
and a lot of players.

00:49:40.175 --> 00:49:41.050
And that's fantastic.

00:49:41.050 --> 00:49:42.910
That's what you want.

00:49:42.910 --> 00:49:44.635
But now you've
got a lot of data.

00:49:44.635 --> 00:49:46.510
You've got a lot of
players and a lot of data

00:49:46.510 --> 00:49:47.927
that you've
accumulated across all

00:49:47.927 --> 00:49:50.030
of these different features.

00:49:50.030 --> 00:49:51.990
So remember the image
of that data center

00:49:51.990 --> 00:49:54.880
that I showed earlier when I
talked about a lot of features

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:57.250
and then I very sneakily
introduced BigQuery?

00:49:57.250 --> 00:50:01.030
Well, here's another example
where BigQuery can help.

00:50:01.030 --> 00:50:03.190
So Firebase and BigQuery
integrate really

00:50:03.190 --> 00:50:04.610
easily with each other.

00:50:04.610 --> 00:50:06.640
And you can export all
those different features

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:08.500
that I just mentioned,
so Analytics

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:12.250
data, Crashlytics data, as
well as Predictions data

00:50:12.250 --> 00:50:14.050
into BigQuery
where you can slice

00:50:14.050 --> 00:50:17.470
and dice it for other insights
that you want to gain.

00:50:17.470 --> 00:50:19.230
So this is really
useful because there's

00:50:19.230 --> 00:50:22.112
some things to these dashboards
themselves might not do

00:50:22.112 --> 00:50:24.570
but that you could write your
own queries against to figure

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:27.130
out all kinds of things.

00:50:27.130 --> 00:50:30.100
Here's some examples.

00:50:30.100 --> 00:50:32.650
So for Analytics, if you
export your Analytics data

00:50:32.650 --> 00:50:36.010
into BigQuery, you can
analyze user retention trends

00:50:36.010 --> 00:50:40.450
for your game and identify
root causes behind them.

00:50:40.450 --> 00:50:44.810
For Crashlytics, you can segment
crashes by levels, libraries,

00:50:44.810 --> 00:50:48.140
or any other custom key that
you include in your crash logs.

00:50:48.140 --> 00:50:50.190
So whatever custom
key you put in there,

00:50:50.190 --> 00:50:52.790
once you've exported your
Crashlytics data into BigQuery,

00:50:52.790 --> 00:50:55.390
you can now use that--
if you'd like to see, OK,

00:50:55.390 --> 00:50:57.200
level 3 has an
issue, and level 3

00:50:57.200 --> 00:51:00.230
is where most of these crashes
are happening-- fairly easily.

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:05.730
And then for Predictions,
one really straightforward

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:09.210
use case and a very common one
is to identify your biggest

00:51:09.210 --> 00:51:14.150
spenders by city or by country
by running a query again

00:51:14.150 --> 00:51:18.180
in BigQuery once you've
exported your data.

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:19.790
So this kind of wraps
up everything we

00:51:19.790 --> 00:51:21.920
wanted to share with you guys.

00:51:21.920 --> 00:51:24.680
We hope this helped you feel
excited about using Firebase

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:25.485
